// The C-string FFI intrinsic. `fromCString` copies a foreign NUL-terminated byte buffer into a
// fresh Ens string. Every call is intercepted by the compiler, so the placeholder body below
// exists only to satisfy the rule that a free function has a body; it never runs.

// A NUL-terminated C string pointer. `fromCString` accepts a value of any external type, so this
// representative handle only names the intrinsic's parameter; the compiler checks the real
// argument's type at each call site, where any external handle (nullable or not) is accepted.
external type CString;

// Copies the NUL-terminated bytes the handle points to into a new Ens string and returns it.
// A null handle yields the empty string "". Freeing the C buffer stays the caller's
// responsibility (for example LLVMDisposeMessage). The compiler replaces every call to this
// function, so the placeholder body is never emitted.
export fromCString(CString handle) -> string {
    return "";
}
